solution:
To find the perimeter of the equilateral triangle whose area is numerically equal to the length of one of its sides, we start with the formula for the area of an equilateral triangle, which is $\frac{s^2 \sqrt{3}}{4}$. Given that the area is numerically equal to the side length $s$, we set the area formula equal to $s$ and solve for $s$:

\begin{align*}
s &= \frac{s^2 \sqrt{3}}{4} \\
\Rightarrow 4s &= s^2\sqrt{3} \\
\Rightarrow 4 &= s\sqrt{3} \\
\Rightarrow \frac{4}{\sqrt{3}} &= s \\
\Rightarrow \frac{4\sqrt{3}}{3} &= s \quad \text{(Multiplying numerator and denominator by $\sqrt{3}$ for rationalization)}
\end{align*}

Now, to find the perimeter of the equilateral triangle, we multiply the side length $s$ by $3$ (since an equilateral triangle has three equal sides):

\begin{align*}
\text{Perimeter} &= 3s \\
&= 3 \cdot \frac{4\sqrt{3}}{3} \\
&= 4\sqrt{3} \text{ units}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the perimeter of the equilateral triangle, in simplest radical form, is $\boxed{4\sqrt{3}}$ units.

